# cool_ideas.txt — the parking lot

Shelved, NOT dead. Each entry: the idea, why it's parked, and what it would touch if revived.
Nothing here is a build target until it's pulled into CLAUDE.md as active work.
This file is a memory aid, not a spec. Design instances only see it if pasted in.

---

## Active-turn / JRPG-tactics combat (the big one)
The enemy acts without waiting for the player to finish their turn — semi-real-time,
ATB-flavored tactics rather than strict I-go-you-go. Not verbatim ATB; the feel is
"the battlefield doesn't freeze while I think."

WHY PARKED: changes EVERYTHING. It breaks the current turn model, the undo system
(you can't freely rewind a clock that's running), and the commit-on-reveal policy.
It is a battle-MODEL decision, not a feature bolt-on.

WHEN IT COMES BACK: as its own dedicated arc, Opus-level, and only after the current
turn-based loop is fully proven and fun. The commitment-policy seam was deliberately
built so "what commits and when" can change — that seam is the hook ATB would use.
Revisit as: "does time pressure inside the battle serve the fantasy, or does it fight
the execute-under-commitment / optimize-between-battles split we chose?"

---

## The emergent nemesis
An ENEMY levy that keeps surviving the player's attacks promotes into a named
antagonist the system generated — "send fifty men to kill me" pointed back at the player.
Tier vocabulary already applies to enemies, so the machinery is half-present.

WHY PARKED: needs recruitment + a persistent campaign force for a nemesis to lead
and recur. Thin without the strategic layer having depth.
WHEN: after campaign recruitment/persistence exists. Highest thematic payoff on the board.

---

## Cards as a lever (economy / logistics)
Shelved from the very start. A card-driven system, most likely thematic home is
economy/logistics rather than combat. Only revive if a REAL thematic home appears —
never as a mechanic for its own sake.

---

## Non-combat third lever (politics / intrigue)
AI-native social conflict as a genuine third pillar alongside tactics and economy —
the "odd trinity." Compelling long-term identity, not a near-term build.

---

## Rest-only healing (attrition tightening)
Make fighting every battle grind a unit down — healing requires resting, so you can't
fight forever without consequence. Currently recovery is uniform/passive.
WHY PARKED: only survivable once recruitment exists to backfill losses; otherwise a
small roster death-spirals. Pairs naturally with the campaign attrition loop.

---

## Terrain-differentiated movement cost
Movement cost varies by tile/terrain in tactical battles. Deferred to keep the movement
economy simple. Pure additive later.

---

## Overnight AI-vs-AI balance sim (NOT really shelved — planned)
Headless, no-render, runs thousands of seeded battles/campaigns to completion and reports
win-rates + outliers. Turns human playtesting into a free overnight process.
Needs: AI that uses the economy, tactics wired into resolveBattle, seeds recorded to state.
This is the payoff of determinism. Build it; don't lose it.
